KERNEL SPACE vs USER SPACE & RING LEVELS

1. User Space
- Normal applications run here (Browser, VS Code, C++ Apps)
- Restricted privileges
- Cannot directly access hardware
- Cannot access kernel memory
- Uses System Calls to request OS services

Examples:
open(), read(), write(), ioctl(), fork()

2. Kernel Space
- Linux Kernel runs here
- Device Drivers run here
- Full access to hardware and memory
- Handles Process Scheduling, Memory Management, File System and Interrupts

Why Separation?
- Security
- Stability
- Prevents applications from crashing the operating system

--------------------------------------------------

RING LEVELS (CPU Privilege Levels)

Ring 0 (Kernel Mode)
- Highest privilege
- Kernel, Device Drivers, ISRs
- Can access hardware directly
- Can execute privileged instructions

Ring 1 & Ring 2
- Available in x86 architecture
- Rarely used by Linux

Ring 3 (User Mode)
- Lowest privilege
- User Applications run here
- Cannot directly access hardware
- Must use System Calls

Linux mainly uses:
Ring 0 = Kernel Space
Ring 3 = User Space

--------------------------------------------------

RING TRANSITION (System Call Flow)

Application (Ring 3)
        |
        | read(), write(), ioctl()
        v
System Call
        |
        v
Kernel (Ring 0)
        |
        v
Device Driver
        |
        v
Hardware
        |
        v
Return to Application (Ring 3)

